Global gate state:
```ts
const gateWanters = new Set<symbol>();
let ownsApexGate = false;
let gateMonitorAttached = false;
function attachGateMonitor(){ if (gateMonitorAttached) return; gateMonitorAttached = true; agentPauseGate.onChange(paused => { if (!paused) { ownsApexGate = false; } }); }
function syncGate(){ attachGateMonitor(); if (gateWanters.size > 0) { if (!agentPauseGate.paused) ownsApexGate = agentPauseGate.pause() || ownsApexGate; else if (ownsApexGate===false && ???) } ... }
```
Careful: if gate paused by the user and we want, we can't own; when the user resumes, `onChange(false)` sets owns=false; our heartbeat re-pauses and sets owns=true. Good. But `syncGate` only called on want changes and heartbeat; the heartbeat calls apply → setWants(want) → syncGate. If wants unchanged (still true), add/delete token does nothing and syncGate isn't called. Need syncGate called on every apply to re-pause after a user resume. So call `syncGate()` unconditionally after updating wanters, or implement `setWants` to always sync. Then on each heartbeat: if wants true and !paused → pause. That matches current behavior.
- If gate paused by the user first: our wants true; sync sees paused → no pause() call; owns stays false. When window ends, wants false → sync sees wanters 0 and owns false → don't resume (user's pause). Correct.
- If gate paused by another peak-gate instance: instance A owns; B wants. If A releases while B wants, sync called from A: wanters still has B → paused → owns stays true (A's owns flag? owns is global, not per instance). Global owns means "the current pause was created by peak-gate". A release shouldn't resume because wanters>0. When B releases (wanters 0), owns true → resume. Good.
- If the user resumes while wanters>0 → onChange(false) sets owns=false; next sync re-pauses and owns=true. Fine.
